# A failed import reads as a working file

**DONE.** `load` refuses a declaration that will not import, and
carries the reason Python gave. It used to answer None, and the
reader fell back to the tree alone.

## Why the fallback was silent

It was written as a principle, and the principle is defensible: a
declaration is a document first, and one that cannot be imported
still parses.

What it did in practice is different. `_resolve` uses the import to
choose between the arms of a `NIX_VERSION` branch, and NO declaration
in this repo has one. So the tree-only read keeps exactly the same
nodes the imported read would have kept, and the file reads as a
working file. Every marker still reads, because markers come off the
tree. Nothing anywhere says a word.

`_resolve` claimed the opposite in its own docstring - "that is loud
rather than silent: the emitter binds it twice and the build says
so". That is true only for a file WITH a branch. For every real
declaration it was silent.

And it spreads. `load` is asked for one file, so the file that fails
reads tree-only and so does every file that imports from it.

## How it was found

`tasks/076`, while writing down what honouring `@property` would
cost. A marker decorator over a `@property` raises while the module
executes:

    AttributeError: 'property' object has no attribute '_instant'
    and no __dict__ for setting new attributes

`@instant`, `@local`, `@reads` and the rest set an attribute on what
they are handed, and a `property` object takes none.

That is the trigger this repo happened to hit. The failure is not
about `@property` at all: ANY import error does it - a typo in an
import, a name a declaration got wrong, a C++ body someone left as
real code.

## What it costs to refuse

Nothing measured. Every one of the fifteen files in `decl/` imports.

The one emitter that already asked the question - `pyerrors.entries`
- refused a missing module itself, "because inventing a hierarchy
would put a wrong answer in four generated files at once". Right, and
the only place that asked. `load` says it for every reader now, and
says WHY, which that refusal could not.

Three optionals went with it, which is the point of doing it here
rather than at each caller: `_live` returns a set, `_resolve` lost
its `live is None` branch, and `Corpus.imported` returns a module.
Each of those was a second definition of what a failed import means.

## The second half: a descriptor with no word

`@staticmethod` and `@classmethod` are refused in `_method` now.

They became reachable in `tasks/075`, when `_live` learnt to look
through a descriptor for its `__code__`. Before that they carried no
live line and were dropped whole, in silence. 075 recorded that the
behaviour had changed and that no gate held it.

Measured with the refusal removed, on a declaration writing
`@staticmethod def of(text: Str) -> Str`:

    method of params [] ret str

The parameter is gone. `_method` reads a bound method by skipping the
first parameter, and a static method's first parameter is not `self`
- so every emitter would write a signature short of an argument.
That is the `open_store(uri)` bug `_method`'s own docstring already
records, reached a second way.

Refused rather than honoured, for the reason `tasks/076` gives about
`@property`: nanobind spells one `def_static` and the other not at
all, the stub needs the same decorator, and `_parts` fetches an
accessor off an INSTANCE. Four outputs for a shape no declaration
wants yet.

`property` is NOT in that list. It is read, into `Method.prop`, and
refused by the emitter that would have to honour it - which is where
the cost is written down.

## Gates

- `test_a_declaration_that_will_not_import_is_refused` asserts both
  halves: that it refuses, and that the message carries Python's own
  reason. A refusal that said only "it did not import" would send a
  reader to the wrong file.
- `test_an_accessor_declared_static_is_refused` covers both
  decorators, on two files - `load` caches by path.

Opened and closed 2026-09-02, out of `tasks/076`.
